Wave 2.5 slice 2 added useMoveEntityV2 as the template; Waves 2.7 and 2.8 followed it verbatim for useInteractV2, useTakeActionV2, useEndTurnV2. Each v1alpha2 unary RPC gets its own hook in src/api/use<Verb>V2.ts:

  export function useMoveEntityV2(): UseMoveEntityV2Result {
    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false)
    const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null)
    const verb = useCallback(async (...) => {
      setLoading(true); setError(null)
      const request = create(<Schema>, {...})
      try { return await encounterClientV2.<verb>(request) }
      catch (err) { const wrapped = err instanceof Error ? err : new Error('<Verb> RPC failed'); setError(wrapped); throw wrapped }
      finally { setLoading(false) }
    }, [])
    return { verb, loading, error }
  }

Invariants honored across all four hooks:
  - loading=true while in-flight, false on resolve/reject
  - error set on failure, cleared on next successful call
  - returned promise rejects on RPC error so callers can surface it
  - request constructed via create(<Schema>, {...}) from @bufbuild/protobuf
  - hook accepts plain JS shapes ({x,y,z}, {module,type,id}) and constructs proto messages internally; passes proto-shaped oneofs (e.g. ActionTarget) through unchanged so the caller controls the case

The client (encounterClientV2 in src/api/client.ts) is constructed from the gen'd v1alpha2 transport, mirroring the v1 client. Remaining v1alpha2 verbs (CreateEncounter, GetEncounter, SubmitCheck) follow the same shape — one file per verb in src/api/.

Wave 2.7 added the OpenDoor section to PlaytestHarness.tsx as a target-input + verb-button pair: a select / text input picks the target ref ({module, type, id}), a button calls the v2 RPC hook (useInteractV2). Wave 2.8 reused the same shape for combat: target-entity input + Attack button (useTakeActionV2) + End Turn button (useEndTurnV2).

The shape:

  function VerbSection({ encounterId, localPlayerId }) {
    const { verb, loading, error } = use<Verb>V2()
    const [target, setTarget] = useState<TargetRef | EntityId>(default)
    const handleClick = async () => {
      try { await verb({ encounterId, target, ...verbSpecific }) }
      catch (e) { /* surfaced via `error` state */ }
    }
    return (
      <section gated-on=relevant-state>
        <select | text-input bound to target />
        <button disabled={loading || !canDo}>{loading ? '...' : 'Verb'}</button>
        {error && <ErrorLine error={error} />}
      </section>
    )
  }

Gating conventions:
  - Move section: always rendered (free-roam works in any mode).
  - Open door section: rendered when at least one door is in state.doors.
  - Combat sections: rendered when mode === 'TURN_BASED' && activeEntityId === localPlayerId.

When Wave 2.9+ adds new Interact targets (chest, lever, trap, NPC dialogue) or new combat actions (cast spell, dash, dodge, second wind), copy this shape — don't invent a new layout per verb. The harness is throwaway scaffolding (per pat-v2-playtest-harness) but consistency makes wave-by-wave verification fast.

Don't promote any of this to durable LobbyView UI. Per feedback_no_logic_in_web, real combat UI in LobbyView (Wave 5+) renders state and dispatches verbs; gating decisions belong on the API.

Wave 2.9 (rpg-dnd5e-web PR #403) established the convention for handling caller-private response payloads (per api-side pat-v2-prompt-on-response). Pattern:

  // useInteractV2 hook returns response unchanged; does not touch encounter state.
  const { interact } = useInteractV2()
  const { setPendingPrompt } = useEncounterState()

  const handleClick = async () => {
    const response = await interact({ encounterId, target })
    if (response.inputRequired) {
      setPendingPrompt(response.inputRequired)
    }
  }

Why NOT auto-set inside the hook:
  - Hook stays pure: input → RPC → output. No coupling to a specific state container.
  - Different callers want different post-response behavior (the playtest harness might also want to log the prompt; a future LobbyView might want to focus the modal; a test might want to assert without side-effects).
  - Single responsibility. The hook does the RPC; the caller decides what to do with caller-private payload.

Same pattern applies to:
  - useTakeActionV2 (Wave 2.9 doesn't wire a prompt-issuing TakeAction yet; future waves will).
  - useSubmitCheckV2.submitCheck() resolution: caller invokes `setPendingPrompt(null)` on success (regardless of roll outcome — success/fail are roll-result, not RPC-result, and both clear the prompt server-side).

When wiring a new RPC whose response carries a side-effect-shaped payload (input_required, narrative_event, prompt_outcome): caller-side hook reads it, caller dispatches the state update. Don't reach into useEncounterState from inside the RPC hook.

Design choice from Wave 2.9 (rpg-dnd5e-web PR #403). The PlaytestHarness's SkillCheckPromptModal renders the SubmitCheck result transiently (e.g. "rolled 14, total 17, success!") for ~2 seconds, then clears the prompt state via `setPendingPrompt(null)`. Naive implementation:

  setTimeout(() => setPendingPrompt(null), 2000)

is a race condition. A new prompt can arrive (alice opens a second locked door, or a future wave introduces a chained-prompt sequence) before the 2s timer fires; the stale timer would then clear the new prompt and the modal would vanish without the user submitting.

Fix: capture a stable identity for the prompt at timer-set time, and check identity before clearing:

  const promptAtSubmit = state.pendingPrompt
  setTimeout(() => {
    // Only clear if the prompt is still the one we just resolved.
    if (state.pendingPrompt === promptAtSubmit) {
      setPendingPrompt(null)
    }
  }, 2000)

Reference identity is sufficient for Wave 2.9 (one prompt slot, prompts replaced wholesale). If a future wave introduces structural-equality concerns, swap to a `promptId` field.

When adding any auto-clear / auto-dismiss timer that mutates encounter state: capture the value-being-cleared at timer-set time, identity-check at timer-fire time. Same pattern fires for: auto-dismissed toast notifications, transient UI overlays, scheduled animations that mutate render state. Reference rpg-dnd5e-web#403's design choice.
